  513|  5.69M|                // But the DFA was reporting [0, 0] and [1, 2]. To understand
  514|  5.69M|                // why this happens, consider the NFA for the aforementioned
  515|  5.69M|                // regex:
  516|  5.69M|                //
  517|  5.69M|                //     >000000: binary-union(4, 1)
  518|  5.69M|                //      000001: \x00-\xFF => 0
  519|  5.69M|                //      000002: WordAscii => 5
  520|  5.69M|                //      000003: % => 5
  521|  5.69M|                //     ^000004: binary-union(2, 3)
  522|  5.69M|                //      000005: binary-union(4, 6)
  523|  5.69M|                //      000006: MATCH(0)
  524|  5.69M|                //
  525|  5.69M|                // The problem here is that one of the DFA start states is
  526|  5.69M|                // going to consist of the NFA states [2, 3] by computing the
  527|  5.69M|                // epsilon closure of state 4. State 4 isn't included because
  528|  5.69M|                // we previously were not keeping track of union states. But
  529|  5.69M|                // only a subset of transitions out of this state will be able
  530|  5.69M|                // to follow WordAscii, and in those cases, the epsilon closure
  531|  5.69M|                // is redone. The only problem is that computing the epsilon
  532|  5.69M|                // closure from [2, 3] is different than computing the epsilon
  533|  5.69M|                // closure from [4]. In the former case, assuming the WordAscii
  534|  5.69M|                // assertion is satisfied, you get: [2, 3, 6]. In the latter
  535|  5.69M|                // case, you get: [2, 6, 3]. Notice that '6' is the match state
  536|  5.69M|                // and appears AFTER '3' in the former case. This leads to a
  537|  5.69M|                // preferential but incorrect match of '%' before returning
  538|  5.69M|                // a match. In the latter case, the match is preferred over
  539|  5.69M|                // continuing to accept the '%'.
