  138|       |            // regex engine itself (assuming a DFA is used). In fact, it is
  139|       |            // usually slower. The magic of Aho-Corasick is that it can search
  140|       |            // a *large* number of literals with a relatively small amount of
  141|       |            // memory. The regex engines are far more wasteful.
  142|       |            //
  143|       |            // Aho-Corasick may be "fast" when the regex engine corresponds
  144|       |            // to, say, the PikeVM. That happens when the lazy DFA couldn't be
  145|       |            // built or used for some reason. But in these cases, the regex
  146|       |            // itself is likely quite big and we're probably hosed no matter
  147|       |            // what we do. (In this case, the best bet is for the caller to
  148|       |            // increase some of the memory limits on the hybrid cache capacity
  149|       |            // and hope that's enough.)
  150|  1.75k|            false
